            <ArticleTitle>A child with abdominal pain and hyperglycemia: is it diabetic ketoacidosis?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>39-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/pec.0b013e31815f6f46</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hyperglycemia, abdominal pain, and vomiting are the most common manifestations of diabetic ketoacidosis in pediatric patients. The absence of ketonemia in these patients should prompt a consideration of acute pancreatitis. We report a case of an 11-year-old girl with acute necrotizing pancreatitis, who was initially diagnosed as having new onset diabetes with nonketotic hyperglycemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
